"""Scene definitions for the Artifact Signing: Signed Releases, Key Rotation and Transparency Logs teaching video (generated from concept.toml)."""

SCENES = [
    dict(key='01-poster', kind='poster', title='Artifact Signing',
         body=None,
         narration="Hello, and welcome. [[slnc 400]] This video explains artifact signing, in Java. [[slnc 300]] This video is presented by Jayasekhar Konduru. [[slnc 600]] First, a simple definition. [[slnc 300]] An artifact is a file that a build produces, such as the jar that a store deploys. [[slnc 300]] Signing it lets every deployment check that the file is exactly what the release team made. [[slnc 600]] Think of a shop that receives sealed crates from its own warehouse. [[slnc 300]] A crate is opened only if the seal is right and unbroken. [[slnc 300]] And every seal ever stamped is written in a public ledger that no one can erase. [[slnc 700]] In this video, the domain is an online store. [[slnc 500]] By the end, you will hear how a release is signed and checked. [[slnc 300]] How signing keys are rotated and revoked. [[slnc 300]] Why the signer's own date cannot be trusted. [[slnc 300]] And how a public log catches a signature made in secret."),
    dict(key='02-scenario', kind='bullets', title='The Scenario',
         body=['An online store builds its release jar.', '', 'It travels through mirrors, caches', 'and build servers to production.', '', 'Any of them could change it.'],
         narration='Here is the scenario. [[slnc 400]] An online store builds its release jar. [[slnc 500]] The jar travels through mirrors, caches, and build servers, on its way to production. [[slnc 500]] Any of them could change it. [[slnc 300]] So production must be able to tell, before it runs anything, whether the file is exactly the one the release team made.'),
    dict(key='03-words', kind='bullets', title='The Words',
         body=['Digest: a fingerprint of exact bytes', 'Signature: proof a key holder vouched', 'Pinned key: the one key a deployment trusts', 'Revocation: a key no longer counts', 'Transparency log: a public, append-only record'],
         narration='Before the demos, the words. [[slnc 500]] A digest is a short fingerprint of the exact bytes of a file. [[slnc 500]] A signature proves that the holder of a key vouched for something. [[slnc 500]] A pinned key is the one key a deployment has decided to trust. [[slnc 500]] Revocation means a key no longer counts. [[slnc 500]] And a transparency log is a public record that can only grow.'),
    dict(key='04-letters-1', kind='bullets', title='What the Letters Stand For',
         body=['SHA, SHA-256: Secure Hash Algorithm; SHA-256 gives 256 bits, 64 hex digits', 'hex: hexadecimal: each byte written as two digits 0-9, a-f', 'Ed25519: Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm on Curve25519', 'jar: Java ARchive: the zip file a Java build produces', 'RFC: Request for Comments: an internet standard document', 'CT: Certificate Transparency: public logs of every web certificate'],
         narration='A few letters come up again and again, so here is what they stand for. [[slnc 400]] S H A is the secure hash algorithm. S H A two fifty six turns any file into a two hundred and fifty six bit digest, written as sixty four hex digits. [[slnc 400]] Hex, short for hexadecimal, writes each byte as two digits from zero to nine and A to F. [[slnc 400]] Ed twenty five five nineteen is the Edwards curve digital signature algorithm, on curve twenty five five nineteen. Its signatures are sixty four bytes. [[slnc 400]] A jar is a Java archive: the zip file that a Java build produces, and that a server runs. [[slnc 400]] An R F C, a request for comments, is an internet standard. R F C ninety one sixty two defines the Merkle tree of certificate transparency. [[slnc 400]] C T is certificate transparency: public, append only logs of every web certificate, built on Merkle trees.'),
    dict(key='05-seal', kind='figure', title='One release, signed and checked',
         body='seal',
         narration="Let's see the core idea on one release. [[slnc 400]] At the top, what the release team does. [[slnc 300]] It takes the jar, and computes the S H A two fifty six digest of its exact bytes. [[slnc 300]] It writes a short statement: the name, the version, the channel, that digest, and the day. [[slnc 300]] And it signs the statement with its private key, the key stable twenty twenty six A. [[slnc 300]] The signature is sixty four bytes. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, what the deployment does. [[slnc 300]] It holds only the matching public key, pinned in advance. [[slnc 300]] It checks the signature, then hashes the jar it actually received. [[slnc 300]] The real jar's digest equals the signed digest: accepted. [[slnc 300]] A jar with one changed byte has a different digest, so it no longer matches: refused. [[slnc 600]] Nobody on the way can fix that, because nobody on the way has the private key."),
    dict(key='06-act-1-figure', kind='figure', title='One bit flipped on the way, and nobody checks',
         body='one_byte',
         narration='First demo: a deployment that runs whatever arrives. [[slnc 400]] On the left, before the mirror: the jar the release team built. [[slnc 300]] It is two thousand four hundred and forty bytes. [[slnc 300]] Byte seven hundred is a slash, hex two F. [[slnc 300]] Its S H A two fifty six digest starts four five seven seven. [[slnc 700]] On the right, after the mirror. [[slnc 300]] The same file name, the same size. [[slnc 300]] But one bit of byte seven hundred was flipped, in amber, so the slash became a dot. [[slnc 300]] And the digest is completely different: it starts six D six C. [[slnc 300]] One bit changes every hex digit. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the trusting deployment step. [[slnc 300]] It never looks at the bytes, so it accepts the altered jar, and runs it in production.'),
    dict(key='07-act-1', kind='console', title='Act One — A deployment that runs whatever arrives',
         body="ONE. A deployment that runs whatever arrives.\n  BEFORE THE MIRROR (what the release team built):\n    file      shop-app-2.4.0.jar, 2440 bytes\n    byte 700  '/' (0x2f)\n    sha256    4577e6b10a2f145d...\n  AFTER THE MIRROR (what the deployment downloaded):\n    file      shop-app-2.4.0.jar, 2440 bytes\n    byte 700  '.' (0x2e)  <- ONE BIT FLIPPED\n    sha256    6d6cca4f654956e0...  <- A NEW DIGEST\n  THE TRUSTING DEPLOYMENT STEP:\n    accepted  deployed shop-app-2.4.0.jar without checking it\n    <- AN ALTERED JAR IS RUNNING\n  WHY A DIGEST NEXT TO THE FILE DOES NOT HELP:\n    whoever can change the file can change the digest beside it",
         narration='Here is the real output. [[slnc 400]] Before the mirror: the file, byte seven hundred, and the digest. [[slnc 300]] After the mirror: the same file name and size, one bit flipped, and a new digest. [[slnc 400]] The trusting step accepted it. [[slnc 300]] An altered jar is running. [[slnc 400]] And a digest printed next to the download does not help. [[slnc 300]] Whoever can change the file can change the digest beside it.'),
    dict(key='08-act-2-figure', kind='figure', title='Six checks in order, and where each attempt stops',
         body='check_grid',
         narration="Second demo: a signed release. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the six checks the deployment runs, in order. [[slnc 300]] Is the statement in its one exact form? [[slnc 300]] Is the key one it pinned? [[slnc 300]] Does the Ed twenty five five nineteen signature verify? [[slnc 300]] Is it for the stable channel? [[slnc 300]] Was the key trusted on the signing day? [[slnc 300]] And does the jar's digest equal the signed digest? [[slnc 700]] On the right, six attempts. [[slnc 300]] A green tick is a check passed, a red cross is where it stops. [[slnc 300]] The real jar passes all six, and is deployed. [[slnc 300]] The jar with one changed byte passes the first five, because the statement is genuine, and stops at the digest. [[slnc 300]] The edited version and the forged key stop at the signature. [[slnc 300]] An unknown key stops at the key, and no signature stops at once."),
    dict(key='09-act-2-1', kind='console', title='Act Two — A signed release, checked before it runs',
         body='TWO. A signed release, checked before it runs.\n  BEFORE SIGNING (the statement, 151 bytes of text):\n    release-statement/v1\n    name=shop-app\n    version=2.4.0\n    channel=stable\n    sha256=4577e6b10a2f145d... (64 hex digits)\n    signed-day=120\n  AFTER SIGNING (Ed25519 with the key stable-2026a):\n    public key  797d4b479b52e839... (32 bytes)\n    signature   6caf4ae3bad1f0aa... (64 bytes)\n    again       the same signature: Ed25519 is deterministic',
         narration='Here is the input: the statement, one hundred and fifty one bytes of text. [[slnc 300]] The name, the version, the channel, the full S H A two fifty six of the jar, and the day. [[slnc 400]] The result: an Ed twenty five five nineteen signature of sixty four bytes, by the key stable twenty twenty six A. [[slnc 300]] Signing again gives the same signature: Ed twenty five five nineteen is deterministic.'),
    dict(key='10-act-2-2', kind='console', title='Act Two — A signed release, checked before it runs',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  HOW THE DEPLOYMENT CHECKS (it pins stable-2026a):\n    1 parse    the statement is in its one exact form\n    2 key      the key id is one the deployment pinned\n    3 verify   Ed25519(public key, statement, signature)\n    4 channel  the key and the statement are for stable\n    5 dates    signed while the key was trusted\n    6 digest   sha256(jar) equals the signed sha256',
         narration='How the deployment checks. [[slnc 300]] It pins the public key of stable twenty twenty six A. [[slnc 300]] Then six checks, in this order, and the first one that fails gives the reason.'),
    dict(key='11-act-2-3', kind='console', title='Act Two — A signed release, checked before it runs',
         body='TWO. (continued)\n  THE RESULTS:\n    the real jar      accepted  signed by stable-2026a\n    one byte changed  refused   jar differs from the signed digest\n    edited to 2.4.1   refused   bad signature\n    a forged key      refused   bad signature\n    an unknown key    refused   unknown key attacker-1\n    no signature      refused   no signature',
         narration='The results. [[slnc 300]] The real jar is accepted. [[slnc 300]] One changed byte: the jar differs from the signed digest. [[slnc 300]] An edited version, or a key that only claims the right name: a bad signature. [[slnc 300]] An unknown key, and no signature, are refused too.'),
    dict(key='12-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='Two keys, an overlap, a revocation and a backdated signature',
         body='key_timeline',
         narration='Third demo: managing the keys. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the two stable keys, day by day. [[slnc 300]] Stable twenty twenty six A is trusted from day zero, until it is revoked on day one hundred and thirty. [[slnc 300]] Stable twenty twenty six B is added on day one hundred. [[slnc 300]] The amber box is the overlap: one hundred and thirty minus one hundred, thirty days with both keys trusted, while every build moves to the new key. [[slnc 700]] Below, signatures, placed on the day they say. [[slnc 300]] Day one hundred and ten, with either key, and day one hundred and twenty: accepted. [[slnc 300]] Day one hundred and forty, after the revocation: refused. [[slnc 700]] At the bottom, the problem. [[slnc 300]] A thief with the old key signs on day one hundred and fifty. [[slnc 300]] But the signer writes the day, so the thief writes day one hundred and twenty. [[slnc 300]] And it is accepted.'),
    dict(key='13-act-3-1', kind='console', title='Act Three — Signing keys: per channel, rotated, revoked',
         body='THREE. Signing keys: per channel, rotated, revoked.\n  A BETA BUILD, OFFERED TO THE STABLE DEPLOYMENT:\n    beta-2026a  refused   beta-2026a signs beta, not stable\n  THE ROTATION (the stable keys, by day):\n    stable-2026a  trusted from day 0, revoked on day 130\n    stable-2026b  added on day 100\n    the overlap   130 - 100 = 30 days, day 100 to 129\n  SIGNED ON A DAY, THE VERDICT:\n    day 110  stable-2026a  accepted  signed by stable-2026a\n    day 110  stable-2026b  accepted  signed by stable-2026b\n    day 120  stable-2026a  accepted  signed by stable-2026a\n    day 140  stable-2026a  refused   signed day 140, revoked day 130',
         narration='First, a beta build offered to the stable deployment. [[slnc 300]] Refused: the beta key signs beta, not stable. [[slnc 400]] Then the rotation. [[slnc 300]] The old key is revoked on day one hundred and thirty, the new one added on day one hundred: a thirty day overlap. [[slnc 400]] Signatures on days one hundred and ten and one hundred and twenty are accepted. [[slnc 300]] Day one hundred and forty is refused.'),
    dict(key='14-act-3-2', kind='console', title='Act Three — Signing keys: per channel, rotated, revoked',
         body='THREE. (continued)\n  BEFORE: A THIEF WITH stable-2026a, ON DAY 150:\n    jar       a different one, sha256 14669a56d76a94fc...\n    writes    signed-day=120, before the revocation\n  AFTER: THE VERDICT:\n    accepted  signed by stable-2026a\n    <- A BACKDATED SIGNATURE: THE SIGNER WRITES THE DAY',
         narration='Before: a thief with the old key, on day one hundred and fifty. [[slnc 300]] A different jar, and the statement says day one hundred and twenty. [[slnc 400]] After: accepted. [[slnc 300]] A backdated signature passes, because the signer writes the day.'),
    dict(key='15-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title="How the log's root is made, and how a fork shows",
         body='merkle_log',
         narration="Fourth demo: a public log. [[slnc 400]] On the left, the log with two entries. [[slnc 300]] Each entry is hashed with a zero byte in front: that is a leaf. [[slnc 300]] Two leaves are hashed together, with a one byte in front: that is the root, C one E eight. [[slnc 300]] The log signs its size and its root: the signed head. [[slnc 300]] To prove that entry one is in the log, it hands out one hash, leaf zero, in amber. [[slnc 300]] Anyone can redo the root from it. [[slnc 700]] On the right, a dishonest log that hides the thief's entry from the store's monitor. [[slnc 300]] It shows the monitor one third entry, and the customer another. [[slnc 300]] Each new root is the old root plus leaf two, so each view still extends the old head. [[slnc 300]] But both are signed, both have size three, and their roots differ. [[slnc 300]] When the monitor and the customer compare heads, that is proof of a fork."),
    dict(key='16-act-4-1', kind='console', title='Act Four — A public log that every signature must be in',
         body="FOUR. A public log that every signature must be in.\n  THE LOG (each entry: key id, statement, signature):\n    entry 0  2.3.0 by stable-2026a, logged day 90, 227 bytes\n    entry 1  2.4.0 by stable-2026a, logged day 120, 228 bytes\n  HOW THE ROOT IS MADE (a Merkle tree, RFC 9162):\n    leaf 0  sha256(00 || entry 0)       = 05e76fd5f6c4ca1a\n    leaf 1  sha256(00 || entry 1)       = cf27ce9d14a8e52d\n    root    sha256(01 || leaf 0 || leaf 1) = c1e815c249fdd097\n  THE SIGNED HEAD:\n    size    2\n    root    c1e815c249fdd097...\n    signed  by the log's own key: true",
         narration='The input: two entries, each with the key id, the statement and the signature. [[slnc 300]] How the root is made: each leaf is the hash of zero and the entry, and the root is the hash of one and both leaves. [[slnc 300]] C one E eight. [[slnc 300]] The log signs that head with its own key.'),
    dict(key='17-act-4-2', kind='console', title='Act Four — A public log that every signature must be in',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  THE PROOF THAT ENTRY 1 IS IN THE LOG (1 hash):\n    proof     leaf 0 = 05e76fd5f6c4ca1a\n    redo      sha256(01 || proof || leaf 1) = c1e815c249fdd097\n    checks    equals the signed root: true\n  THE DEPLOYMENT, NOW REQUIRING A LOG ENTRY:\n    the real 2.4.0    accepted  signed by stable-2026a, entry 1\n    thief, not logged refused   not in the public log\n    thief, logged     refused   logged day 150, revoked day 130\n    <- THE LOG'S DAY COUNTS, NOT THE DAY THE SIGNER WROTE\n  THE STORE'S MONITOR (signed with its keys, never released):\n    entry 2: 2.4.0 by stable-2026a, sha256 14669a56d76a94fc",
         narration="The proof for entry one is one hash, leaf zero. [[slnc 300]] Hashed with leaf one, it gives the signed root again. [[slnc 400]] Now the deployment requires a log entry. [[slnc 300]] The real release is accepted as entry one. [[slnc 300]] The thief's jar is refused unlogged, and refused when logged, by the log's day, one hundred and fifty. [[slnc 300]] And the store's monitor reports entry two, a release the store never made."),
    dict(key='18-act-4-3', kind='console', title='Act Four — A public log that every signature must be in',
         body="FOUR. (continued)\n  A LOG THAT HIDES ENTRY 2 SHOWS TWO VIEWS OF SIZE 3:\n    monitor   entry 2 = the beta build, root f230f49e340792fc\n    customer  entry 2 = the thief's jar, root 92f28850530761dd\n  HOW EACH VIEW EXTENDS THE HEAD OF SIZE 2 (proof: leaf 2):\n    monitor   sha256(01 || c1e815c2 || 73de63d6) = f230f49e340792fc\n    customer  sha256(01 || c1e815c2 || 9386a157) = 92f28850530761dd\n    both      consistent with the old head: true and true\n  THE MONITOR AND THE CUSTOMER COMPARE HEADS:\n    same size  3 = 3, but two different roots, both signed\n    fork       proved: true  <- ONE LOG, TWO HISTORIES",
         narration='A log that hides entry two from the monitor shows two views of size three. [[slnc 300]] Each root is the old root hashed with a different leaf two, so both views extend the old head. [[slnc 300]] But one size with two signed roots proves the fork.'),
    dict(key='19-act-5-figure', kind='figure', title='What the gate guards, and what it costs',
         body='gate_paths',
         narration='Fifth demo: the bill. [[slnc 400]] At the top, the path a release takes. [[slnc 300]] It is built, signed with a key in hardware, written to the public log, checked by the gate, and only then run on the server. [[slnc 700]] The red dashed line is the problem. [[slnc 300]] A jar copied by hand straight onto a server never meets the gate. [[slnc 300]] The gate refuses an unsigned jar, but only on its own path, so every other path must be locked. [[slnc 700]] Then the key. [[slnc 300]] Here it comes from a fixed demo seed. [[slnc 300]] In practice it lives in a hardware module, and every use is approved. [[slnc 300]] And rotation needs an overlap, so every deployment trusts the new key before the old one goes.'),
    dict(key='20-act-5', kind='console', title='Act Five — The bill',
         body='FIVE. The bill.\n  THE GATE GUARDS ONE PATH:\n    an unsigned jar   refused   no signature\n    copied by hand    never meets the gate: lock the other paths\n  THE SIGNING KEY IS THE CROWN JEWEL:\n    here         made from a fixed demo seed\n    in practice  in a hardware module; each use approved\n  ROTATION IS A PROJECT:\n    every deployment trusts the new key before the old one\n    is revoked: here an overlap of 30 days\n  KEYLESS SIGNING (the -with-cosign version):\n    short-lived certificates tied to an identity, in a public log',
         narration='The gate refuses an unsigned jar. [[slnc 300]] But a jar copied by hand never meets it. [[slnc 400]] The signing key is the crown jewel: in practice, in a hardware module. [[slnc 400]] Rotation needs an overlap of thirty days here. [[slnc 400]] And keyless signing replaces long lived keys with short lived certificates, tied to an identity and recorded in a public log. [[slnc 300]] That is the cosign version of this project.'),
    dict(key='21-ladder', kind='bullets', title='Four Questions Before Anything Runs',
         body=['Are these the exact bytes? the digest', 'Who vouched for them? the signature', 'Was that key trusted then? channel, rotation, revocation', 'Could anyone see the signing? the log'],
         narration="Let's put it in order. [[slnc 400]] Are these the exact bytes the release team made? [[slnc 300]] The digest answers that. [[slnc 500]] Who vouched for them? [[slnc 300]] The signature. [[slnc 500]] Was that key trusted for this channel, on that day? [[slnc 300]] The key ring. [[slnc 500]] And could anyone see that the signing happened? [[slnc 300]] The transparency log."),
    dict(key='22-recognise', kind='bullets', title='Where You Have Seen It',
         body=['Package managers checking signatures', '.sig and .asc files next to downloads', 'Certificate Transparency logs', 'Supply chain incidents'],
         narration='You have probably met this already. [[slnc 400]] Package managers check a signature before they install anything. [[slnc 500]] Downloads often come with a signature file, and a published key. [[slnc 500]] Every web certificate is recorded in public certificate transparency logs. [[slnc 500]] And supply chain incidents often begin with a release that was changed on the way.'),
    dict(key='23-verdict', kind='bullets', title='How To Use It',
         body=['Sign a statement over the digest', 'Verify at the point of use, keys pinned per channel', 'Keep keys in hardware; rotate with an overlap', 'Revoke by an independent time', 'Require the log, and watch it'],
         narration='So, how do you use it? [[slnc 400]] Sign a statement over the digest of every release. [[slnc 500]] Verify it where the artifact is used, with keys pinned per channel. [[slnc 500]] Keep signing keys in hardware, and rotate them with an overlap. [[slnc 500]] Revoke by a time that the signer cannot write. [[slnc 500]] And require every signature to be in a public log, and watch that log for releases you never made.'),
    dict(key='24-classes', kind='bullets', title='The Java Classes',
         body=['Statement: what a signature vouches for, in one exact form', 'SigningKey: a key with an id and the one channel it signs for', 'Verifier: the checks, in order; the first that fails gives the reason', 'KeyRing: the pinned public keys, per channel, with their days', "MerkleTree: the log's tree, as RFC 9162 defines it", "DeployGate: the point of use: signature, log entry, and the log's day", "Monitor: the store's watcher of the public log"],
         narration='Now, a quick look at the code. [[slnc 300]] Each class does one step of the checks you just saw. [[slnc 300]] Here are the lines that matter.'),
    dict(key='25-class-1', kind='code', title='Statement — the bytes that are signed',
         body='// Statement: what a signature vouches for, in one exact form\nbyte[] bytes() {\n    String text = HEADER + "\\n"\n            + "name=" + name + "\\n"\n            + "version=" + version + "\\n"\n            + "channel=" + channel + "\\n"\n            + "sha256=" + sha256 + "\\n"\n            + "signed-day=" + signedDay + "\\n";',
         narration='A statement holds the name, the version, the channel, the digest of the jar, and the day. [[slnc 300]] Its text has one fixed form, so the same statement always gives the same bytes, and the same signature.'),
    dict(key='26-class-2', kind='code', title="SigningKey — sign the statement's bytes",
         body='// SigningKey: a key with an id and the one channel it signs for\nSignedRelease sign(Statement statement) {\n    byte[] bytes = statement.bytes();\n    return new SignedRelease(bytes, keyId, Ed25519.sign(pair.getPrivate(), bytes));',
         narration="Signing takes the statement's bytes and signs them with the private key. [[slnc 300]] The result travels with the jar: the statement, the key id, and the signature."),
    dict(key='27-class-3', kind='code', title='Verifier — the key, the signature, the dates, the digest',
         body='// Verifier: the checks, in order; the first that fails gives the reason\nKeyRing.TrustedKey key = keys.find(release.keyId()).orElse(null);\nif (key == null) {\n    return Verdict.refuse("unknown key " + release.keyId());\n...\nif (!Ed25519.verify(key.publicKey(), release.statement(), release.signature())) {\n    return Verdict.refuse("bad signature");\n...\nif (statement.signedDay() >= key.revokedFromDay()) {\n...\nif (!Digests.same(Digests.unhex(statement.sha256()), artifact.sha256())) {\n    return Verdict.refuse("jar differs from the signed digest");',
         narration="The verifier looks up the key by its id in the pinned key ring. [[slnc 300]] It checks the signature over the exact statement bytes. [[slnc 300]] Then the day against the revocation, and last, the jar's own digest against the signed one."),
    dict(key='28-class-4', kind='code', title='KeyRing — revocation counts from a day',
         body='// KeyRing: the pinned public keys, per channel, with their days\nvoid revoke(String keyId, int fromDay) {\n    TrustedKey key = keys.get(keyId);\n    keys.put(keyId, new TrustedKey(key.keyId(), key.channel(), key.publicKey(),\n            key.validFromDay(), fromDay));',
         narration='Revoking a key does not delete it. [[slnc 300]] It records the day from which its signatures stop counting, so older releases still deploy.'),
    dict(key='29-class-5', kind='code', title='MerkleTree — a leaf and a node are hashed differently',
         body="// MerkleTree: the log's tree, as RFC 9162 defines it\nprivate static final byte[] LEAF = {0x00};\nprivate static final byte[] NODE = {0x01};\n...\nstatic byte[] leafHash(byte[] data) {\n    return Digests.sha256(LEAF, data);\n...\nstatic byte[] nodeHash(byte[] left, byte[] right) {\n    return Digests.sha256(NODE, left, right);",
         narration='A leaf is the hash of a zero byte and the entry. [[slnc 300]] A node is the hash of a one byte and its two children. [[slnc 300]] The different first byte means a leaf can never pass for a node.'),
    dict(key='30-class-6', kind='code', title="DeployGate — the log's day, not the signer's",
         body='// DeployGate: the point of use: signature, log entry, and the log\'s day\nint index = log.indexOf(release);\nif (index < 0) {\n    return Verdict.refuse("not in the public log");\n...\nint loggedDay = log.dayOf(index);\nif (loggedDay >= key.revokedFromDay()) {',
         narration='After the signature checks, the gate requires the entry in the log, with a proof against the signed head. [[slnc 300]] Then it checks the revocation against the day the log recorded. [[slnc 300]] That is what stops the backdated signature.'),
    dict(key='31-class-7', kind='code', title='Monitor — a store key, an unknown digest',
         body='// Monitor: the store\'s watcher of the public log\nif (!storeKeys.isStoreKey(keyId)) {\n    continue;\n...\nif (!knownDigests.contains(digest)) {\n    findings.add("entry " + i + ": " + version + " by " + keyId\n            + ", sha256 " + digest.substring(0, 16));',
         narration="The monitor reads every entry. [[slnc 300]] An entry signed with one of the store's keys, for a digest the store never released, is reported."),
    dict(key='32-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Make the gate refuse', 'a rollback to an older signed jar.'],
         narration="That's artifact signing. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Run only the exact bytes that a trusted key signed, and only if that signature is in a public log that someone watches. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine, with nothing installed except a Java development kit. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Make the gate refuse a rollback to an older signed jar. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
]

